; Albion's RunProcECX (games/Albion/SR-Main/llasm/Game-llasm.llasm), purely
; generic. A guest "address of proc" value
; (e.g. the handler the game hands INT 21h AH=25h) is a real, directly
; callable host address here: the executable links with a fixed
; --Ttext-segment precisely so translated procs live at stable addresses, and
; llasm's own calling convention (fastcc on the single %_cpu* argument) makes
; a bare indirect tail call through a register ABI-correct. MD-runtime.c uses
; this to actually run the guest's registered timer ISR from
; call_guest_interrupt_vector, instead of synthesizing an x86 interrupt frame
; (which crashed -- see MD-runtime.c's comment there).

include llasm.llinc

proc RunProcECX global c_RunProcECX

        tcall ecx

endp ; end procedure RunProcECX

; The default handler for interrupt vectors nothing has registered.
; WATCOM/DOS4GW startup code (like the game's own INT 08h handler) commonly
; chains to "the previously installed handler" by overwriting its own return
; frame with the saved old vector and executing retf/iret into it -- real DOS
; always has *some* handler there (the BIOS's own default), never a null
; pointer. MD-runtime.c seeds interrupt_vectors[] with this stub's address
; instead of leaving DOS_getvect's answer as 0, so that chain lands somewhere
; safe (it returns at once, as SR translates iretd -- see
; SR_full_llasm_instr.c's UD_Iiretd case) instead of jumping to address 0.
; An ISR must leave every guest register exactly as it found it, so the return
; address goes in a scratch temp, not in eax -- byte-for-byte the same sequence
; SR emits for the game's own `iretd` sites (loc_107C3, loc_5405E, loc_688EC,
; loc_688F3).
proc md_null_isr global c_md_null_isr

        add tmpadr, esp, 8
        load tmp1, tmpadr, 4
        PUSH tmp1
        POPFD
        POP tmp1
        add esp, esp, 8
        tcall tmp1

endp ; end procedure md_null_isr

; Stands in for the three-byte `int N / ret` stub of WATCOM's int386().
; loc_74928 dispatches a software interrupt by loading the caller's register
; block and *returning* into one of 256 such stubs in the table at loc_749CE,
; indexed base + N*3. SR has to leave that table as raw data -- translating
; `int N` into a call to x86_int would destroy the three-byte stride every
; index depends on -- so returning into it would jump straight into data.
; instruction_replacements.sci rewrites loc_74928 to stash N in md_int86_no and
; push this proc's address instead of &stub[N]. The stack effect is the stub's:
; x86_int cleans up after itself, so the final pop-and-tail-call lands on
; int386's continuation exactly as the real `ret` would have.
extern md_int86_no

proc md_int86_thunk public

        load tmp1, md_int86_no, 4
        call x86_int $, tmp1

        POP tmp1
        tcall tmp1

endp ; end procedure md_int86_thunk
